  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Gender and Christianity in Modern Europe shows a clear early scholarly footprint and sustained reader interest. The book has 5 OpenAlex citations and 3,876 recorded OAPEN downloads, indicating that it is being used both as a research reference and as a widely accessed open book. Its thematic alignment with gender equality also points to policy and sustainability relevance.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Patrick Pasture’s Gender and Christianity in Modern Europe, published by Leuven University Press in 2012, examines the long-running “feminization thesis” in the history of Christianity and tests it against concepts such as masculinity, religion, and gendered social change. The record shows a work positioned at the intersection of gender studies, religious studies, sociology, and modern European history, with a focus that resonates with the SDG area of gender equality.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest evidence of reach is usage: the book has been downloaded 3,876 times in the OAPEN environment, which suggests substantial reader engagement with the open version. This is complemented by OpenAIRE records showing the book is openly available, supporting access beyond a narrow subscription audience. Taken together, the usage record points to a work that has been actively consulted rather than remaining only on library shelves.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Citation evidence is modest but real. OpenAlex records 5 citations, with citing works appearing across 2013, 2018, and 2020. The citing literature is varied, spanning social work, population studies, and historical scholarship, including work on masculinity, fertility, and religious sociability. That pattern suggests the book has entered adjacent scholarly conversations where gender and religion are being used to interpret broader social and historical questions.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The surrounding record is thinner on other forms of documented public attention: no event archive is available, and no additional community discourse is captured here. In that context, the book’s impact story is best understood as a combination of open-access reach, early but cross-disciplinary scholarly uptake, and conceptual relevance to ongoing debates about gender, religion, and modernity.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the evidence portrays a book with visible academic use and a durable topical fit. Its open availability appears to have supported readership, while the citation record shows that its arguments have been taken up in subsequent research across multiple fields.</p>",
